 17592186045585 true] [17592186386504 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =3[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/7165c52d2280e4a53dc640acdca7671dcb897921028e675a583c46e6ccd40e33.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386506 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386507 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386508 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386509 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386510 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1079 #edb/ref 17592186386506 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386505 1080 #uuid "4d363d95-cb90-5aa7-