e] [17592186386773 1074 "Since the hedgehog population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hedgehog population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hedgehog population, the hedgehog population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000.$ Therefore, the hedgehog population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(50\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(50\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=12\\,500$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =12\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.25(0)}} & =12\\,500 \\\\ 1+C & =4 \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.25\\cdot t}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=20$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(20) & =\\frac{50\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.25\\cdot 20}} \\\\  & ≈49\\,009.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386775 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386776 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386777 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386778 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386779 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386774 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186