& \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386833 1072 #uuid "3e35edc1-4549-5760-a079-c70fcb33349e" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386833 1074 "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\n{R}_{\\theta }=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta <0$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta >0,$  we must have that $\\theta $ lies in $4$th quadrant. \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians *clockwise* about the origin.\n\nFinally, to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ *clockwise* by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a9/a9db712576dc02afa62ae58792428258c0b3263ac2c81b34db1be2633ec0b57e.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386835 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386836 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386837 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386838 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386839 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386834 1079 #edb/ref 1